##  Method Overloading (Compile-time Polymorphism)
Defining multiple methods with the same name but different parameters.

* Method name must be same
* Parameters must be different (type / number / order)
* Return type alone cannot differentiate

### Example

class Calculator {

    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    int add(int a, int b, int c) {
        return a + b + c;
    }
}



## Method Overriding (Runtime Polymorphism)

A subclass provides a specific implementation of a method already defined in parent class.

* Same method name
* Same parameters
* Requires inheritance
* Uses runtime polymorphism

### Example

class Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal makes sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}



## Key Differences: Overloading vs Overriding

| Feature            | Method Overloading                 | Method Overriding        |
| ------------------ | ---------------------------------- | ------------------------ |
| Definition         | Same method name, different params | Redefining parent method |
| Polymorphism Type  | Compile-time                       | Runtime                  |
| Inheritance Needed | No                                 | Yes                      |
| Parameters         | Must differ                        | Must be same             |



